# Momen.app Actionflow

## Overview
Although momen.app already supports direct CRUD operations that can be initiated from the frontend, many backend operations are multi-step, can be long-running and sometimes have to be asynchronous. Therefore momen.app also supports actionflows for these scenarios. An actionflow is a directed acyclic graph made up of actionflow nodes. These nodes represent either operations (e.g. insert into database, invoke another actionflow) or control flow changes (condition and loop). Actionflows also have two special nodes, input and output, where the arguments and return values of the entire actionflow are defined. 

Actionflows have two modes of operation, sync or async. A synchronous Actionflow waits for its result. Only database-operation nodes participate in the database transaction: they commit together on success and roll back together on failure. API, AI, payment, file, and other external operations stay outside that transaction and do not roll back automatically. Synchronous Actionflows are subject to the synchronous runtime cap. An asynchronous Actionflow runs in the background and does not provide one transaction across all database nodes. It is suited to longer-running work, such as long HTTP calls and LLM API calls. AI Agent nodes can only be added inside asynchronous Actionflows. 

## Actionflow invocation process
In order to invoke an actionflow, obtain its id and arguments from the project schema. Current integrations call the deployed Actionflow with `fz_invoke_action_flow_default_by_latest_version` or `fz_create_action_flow_task`, passing `actionFlowId` and `args`. The `versionId` argument and `fz_invoke_action_flow` remain in the schema only for existing integrations. Do not pass an Actionflow version in a new integration.
Actionflow invocations differ based on their type. 

### Sync actionflows
Sync actionflows can be invoked via a regular GraphQL mutation. The results will be returned in the response of the same HTTP request. 
Request:
```gql
mutation InvokeActionFlow($actionFlowId: String!, $args: Json!) {
  fz_invoke_action_flow_default_by_latest_version(
    actionFlowId: $actionFlowId
    args: $args
  )
}
```

```json
{
  "actionFlowId": "d3ea4f95-5d34-46e1-b940-91c4028caff5",
  "args": {
    "yaml": "post_link:\n  url: \"https://momen.app\"\n",
    "img_id": 1020000000000111
  }
}
```
Within this query, $args corresponds to the arguments listed in the actionflow's input node. 
Response:
```json
{
  "data": {
    "fz_invoke_action_flow_default_by_latest_version": {
      "img": {
        "id": 1020000000000090,
        "url": "https://fz-zion-static.functorz.com/202510252359/a64a7eb4793728a1977d3ea9e7b7e4e8/project/2000000000521152/import/1110000000000001/image/636.jpg"
      },
      "url": "https://momen.app"
    }
  }
}
```

### Async actionflows
Async actionflows are triggered via a GraphQL mutation but the results are not returned in the response of the same HTTP request. Instead, a fz_create_action_flow_task is returned, containing the id of the corresponding task, which is then used for subscribing to the result in a separate GraphQL subscription. 
Mutation request:
```gql
mutation CreateActionFlowTask($actionFlowId: String!, $args: Json!) {
  fz_create_action_flow_task(actionFlowId: $actionFlowId, args: $args)
}
```

```json
{
  "actionFlowId": "2a9068c5-8ee3-4dad-b3a4-5f3a6d365a2f",
  "args": {
    "int": 123,
    "img_id": 1020000000000116,
    "some_text": "Dreamer",
    "datetime_with_timezone": "2025-10-23T20:13:00-07:00"
  }
}
```
Mutation response:
```json
{
  "data": {
    "fz_create_action_flow_task": 1150000000000148
  }
}
```

Subscription request: 
```gql
subscription fz_listen_action_flow_result($taskId: Long!) {
  fz_listen_action_flow_result(taskId: $taskId) {
    __typename
    output
    status
  }
}
```
```json
{ "taskId" : 1150000000000148 }
```
Subscription response:
```json
{
  "data": {
    "fz_listen_action_flow_result": {
      "__typename": "ActionFlowTaskResult",
      "output": {
        "img": {
          "id": 1020000000000089,
          "url": "https://fz-zion-static.functorz.com/202510262359/3a5f04371bf68d6c94bb890879101f0a/project/2000000000521152/import/1110000000000001/image/637.jpg"
        },
        "xyz": {
          "type": "Point",
          "coordinates": [
            131,
            22
          ]
        }
      },
      "status": "COMPLETED"
    }
  }
}
```
There might be multiple messages sent by the GraphQL subscription before the final result (inside "output") is returned, and each may contain different status values.
If the client cannot hold a WebSocket, poll the same taskId. The query returns the current snapshot and does not replay intermediate updates:
```gql
query GetActionFlowResult($taskId: Long!) {
  fz_action_flow_result(taskId: $taskId) {
    status
    output
  }
}
``` 
The status field uses the TaskStatus enum: CREATED, PROCESSING, COMPLETED, and FAILED.
